"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][3]={"university_physics_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – Kinematics in One and Two Dimensions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Vectors, derivatives, elementary\nintegrals and SI units. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish vector position,\nvelocity and acceleration and calculate projectile motion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Describe Motion Without Specifying Its Cause<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Kinematics<\/strong> describes how a body’s position changes\nwith time; the forces responsible are addressed later in dynamics. In\nthe point-particle model, position is a vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf r(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith components depending on the selected reference frame. The\ndisplacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\mathbf r=\\mathbf r(t_2)-\\mathbf r(t_1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis different from the distance travelled: after one complete circle the\ndisplacement vanishes, but the path length does not.\n<strong>Velocity<\/strong> and <strong>acceleration<\/strong> are the time\nderivatives of position and velocity, respectively. Motion in two or\nthree dimensions can therefore be analysed component by component.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differentiating and Integrating Motion<\/h2>\n<p>In a chosen inertial frame, the fundamental relations are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf v(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm d\\mathbf r}{\\mathrm dt},\\qquad\n\\mathbf a(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm d\\mathbf v}{\\mathrm dt},\\qquad\n\\Delta\\mathbf r=\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\mathbf v(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For constant acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nintegrating twice gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf v(t)=\\mathbf v_0+\\mathbf a t,\\qquad\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf r_0+\\mathbf v_0t+\\tfrac12\\mathbf a t^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The expression uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the chosen starting time; for a different starting instant, replace\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t-t_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDimensional analysis checks the terms: both\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf v_0t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\mathbf a t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave units of length. A negative component specifies direction relative\nto an axis, not necessarily a reduction in speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Oblique Projectile<\/h2>\n<p>A ball is launched from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_0=20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npoint horizontally and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nupward. Neglect drag and assume constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>g<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=v_0\\cos\\alpha\\,t,\\qquad\ny(t)=v_0\\sin\\alpha\\,t-\\tfrac12gt^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Setting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(T)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the nonzero flight time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2v_0\\sin\\alpha/g\\approx2.04\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>35.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=v_0\\cos\\alpha\\,T\\approx35.3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nmaximum height occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curved path does not require a horizontal force. It results from the\nsuperposition of constant horizontal velocity and accelerated vertical\nmotion.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_225_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabolic projectile path with initial velocity and components\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parabolic projectile path with initial\nvelocity and components<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In ideal projectile motion horizontal velocity is constant while\nvertical velocity decreases.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Position is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=2t^2-3t+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmetres with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin seconds. Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the projectile above, find the initial velocity in the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirection and the instant when it vanishes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the magnitude of average velocity not always the average\nspeed?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)=4t-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(t)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(2)=5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a(2)=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{0y}=20\\sin30^\\circ=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_y=10-9.81t=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\approx1.02\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Average vector velocity uses displacement, whereas average speed\nuses total distance travelled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Scope and Choice of Frame<\/h2>\n<p>The constant-acceleration equations do not describe every motion. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvaries with time or position, one must integrate the corresponding\nfunction or solve an equation of motion. Drag, wind and changing\naltitude modify projectile results. Measurements should therefore begin\nby specifying axes, origin, units and modelling assumptions. A\npassenger’s velocity relative to a train differs from their velocity\nrelative to the platform; the reference frame is part of a meaningful\nphysical statement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Kinematics links observations to mathematical models of motion. Next\nwe explain acceleration in terms of forces.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Newton’s Laws and Free-Body\nDiagrams<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-introduction\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 3 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter Outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L1 – Kinematics in One and Two Dimensions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-introduction\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 4 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter Outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L1 – Kinematics in One and Two Dimensions · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – Newton’s Laws and Free-Body Diagrams<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Vector kinematics and component\ncalculations from M3-L1. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply Newton’s laws to force\nsystems and construct correct free-body diagrams.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Motion to Interaction<\/h2>\n<p>Dynamics identifies the <strong>interactions<\/strong> that produce\nacceleration. Forces are vectors, measured in newtons:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNewton’s first law expresses inertia: in an inertial reference frame, an\nobject keeps constant velocity when the net force vanishes. Thus\n<strong>zero acceleration<\/strong> does not imply an absence of forces;\nseveral forces may cancel. The second law relates the sum of external\nforces to the rate of momentum change. For constant mass, this becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum\\mathbf F=m\\mathbf a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three Laws and the System Boundary<\/h2>\n<p>For a body of constant mass, Newton’s second law reads<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum\\mathbf F_{\\mathrm{ext}}=m\\mathbf a.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Newton’s third law pairs forces as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf F_{A\\to B}=-\\mathbf F_{B\\to A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese two forces act <strong>on different bodies<\/strong>; they must not\nbe cancelled in the free-body diagram of one body. A free-body diagram\nisolates the chosen object, shows every external force with its\ndirection and point of application and represents contacts by forces.\nChoosing suitable axes then gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum F_x=ma_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum F_y=ma_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear Earth’s surface the weight has magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na normal force is a contact force and need not equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Crate on a Smooth Floor<\/h2>\n<p>A crate of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=5.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis pulled to the right across a horizontal, frictionless floor by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=20\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nVertically, the forces balance because the crate does not lift off:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>49.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N-mg=0,\\qquad N=49.05\\,\\mathrm N.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Horizontally the applied force is the net force:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>F<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_x=\\frac{F}{m}=\\frac{20\\,\\mathrm N}{5.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}}=4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The free-body diagram shows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nupward,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndownward and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the right. Acceleration is not an additional force on the diagram.\nStarting at rest, the crate reaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_106_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Crate on a horizontal surface showing three external force arrows\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Crate on a horizontal surface showing\nthree external force arrows<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Normal force balances weight vertically; the pull accelerates\nhorizontally.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A horizontal net force of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the acceleration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A block rests on a table. Which external forces belong in its\nfree-body diagram?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na Newton’s-third-law pair? Explain.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=15/3.0=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Weight downward and the normal force upward, assuming no other\ncontact forces.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Both forces act on the same object. The reaction to the normal\nforce acts on the table; the reaction to the gravitational force acts on\nEarth.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Common Modelling Errors<\/h2>\n<p>A free-body diagram contains only forces acting on the selected\nobject, not arrows for its velocity or forces exerted by it on its\nsurroundings. The mass may be treated as constant in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=ma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly if the definition of the system supports that assumption.\nAccelerating reference frames require additional inertial-force terms in\ntheir description; the basic form of Newton’s laws assumes an inertial\nframe. Friction, tension and drag must be introduced as separate force\nmodels when relevant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Forces must be assigned carefully to bodies and directions. The next\nlesson covers friction, normal forces and circular motion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Friction, Normal Forces and\nCircular Motion<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-1-forces\">University Physics Volume 1 – 5.1 Forces<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 5.1, learning objectives and introduction; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L2 – Newton’s Laws and Free-Body Diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-1-solving-problems-with-newtons-laws\">University Physics Volume 1 – 6.1 Solving Problems with Newton’s Laws<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.1, Problem-Solving Strategies; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L2 – Newton’s Laws and Free-Body Diagrams · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Friction, Normal Forces and Circular Motion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Newton’s laws and free-body diagrams\nfrom M3-L2. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish static and kinetic\nfriction and solve radial force balances for circular motion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Contact and Change of Direction<\/h2>\n<p>A moving body can accelerate even at constant speed: on a circular\npath the <strong>direction<\/strong> of its velocity vector changes\ncontinuously. For radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand constant speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe radial acceleration points toward the centre. “Centripetal force” is\nnot an additional fundamental force; it names the <strong>net radial\ncomponent<\/strong> of the actual forces. Tension, gravity, static\nfriction or combinations of forces can supply it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Static Friction, Kinetic Friction and Radial Equation<\/h2>\n<p>In the simple Coulomb model, the magnitude of static friction is\nbounded, whereas kinetic friction during sliding is approximately\nproportional to the normal force:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|F_{\\mathrm H}|\\le\\mu_{\\mathrm H}N,\\qquad\n|F_{\\mathrm G}|\\approx\\mu_{\\mathrm G}N.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm H}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm G}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare dimensionless and depend on surfaces and conditions. Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the force balance normal to the surface, rather than assuming it\nalways equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor uniform circular motion,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mfrac><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_r=\\frac{v^2}{R}=R\\omega^2,\\qquad \\sum F_{\\mathrm{rad}}=m\\frac{v^2}{R}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Static friction opposes impending relative motion; kinetic friction\nopposes actual relative sliding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: A Flat Road Curve<\/h2>\n<p>A car takes a flat curve of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=50\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a simplified model, static friction between tyres and road supplies\nthe radial force. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm H}=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand vertical equilibrium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mv^2/R\\le\\mu_{\\mathrm H}mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.60<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>17.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\max}=\\sqrt{\\mu_{\\mathrm H}gR}\n=\\sqrt{0.60\\cdot9.81\\cdot50}\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\n\\approx17.2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>61.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">61.8\\,\\mathrm{km/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mass cancels in this idealised model. The diagram shows inward\nradial acceleration and velocity tangent to the circular path.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_039_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circular path with tangential velocity and radial acceleration arrows\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Circular path with tangential velocity\nand radial acceleration arrows<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Velocity is tangent to the path; the required acceleration points\ninward.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find radial acceleration at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=25\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A block on level ground has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=100\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_{\\mathrm H}=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is the maximum static friction?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the static-friction force not automatically\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhorizontal pull?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_r=v^2/R=4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm H,max}=0.4\\cdot100=40\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Static friction adjusts up to its limit; if equilibrium is possible,\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nopposite the pull.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limitations and Real-World Safety<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation is not a driving recommendation: friction\ncoefficient, water film, tyres, banking, road conditions and driver\nactions vary. Nor does a new real outward force act on the car in the\ninertial frame. Such language often results from silently switching to a\nrotating vehicle frame. A reliable solution first draws actual forces,\nselects “toward the centre” as the radial direction and only then tests\nthe static-friction inequality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Friction is a contact force with a static limit; circular motion\nrequires a radial force balance. We now turn to energy methods.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Work, Energy and\nPotential<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-1-solving-problems-with-newtons-laws\">University Physics Volume 1 – 6.1 Solving Problems with Newton’s Laws<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.1, Problem-Solving Strategies; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L3 – Friction, Normal Forces and Circular Motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-3-centripetal-force\">University Physics Volume 1 – 6.3 Centripetal Force<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.3, equations for radial acceleration; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L3 – Friction, Normal Forces and Circular Motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 6 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 6.1; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L3 – Friction, Normal Forces and Circular Motion · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Work, Energy and Potential<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Newton’s laws, the dot product and\nintegration. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Relate work to kinetic energy\nand use conservative potentials to analyse motion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Energy Is Another Language of Mechanics<\/h2>\n<p>Forces describe instantaneous acceleration, while\n<strong>energy<\/strong> often connects initial and final states\ndirectly. Work is not simply a force magnitude times an arbitrary\ndistance; it is the line integral of the force component along the\nactual displacement. For a constant-mass point particle, the total work\nequals the change in kinetic energy. The energy approach may avoid\nsolving a full time-dependent trajectory, although energy alone\ngenerally does not give the travel time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Work–Energy Theorem and Conservative Forces<\/h2>\n<p>For a force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacting along a path\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r,\\qquad\nW_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\Delta K,\\qquad K=\\tfrac12 mv^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In a suitable domain, a force is <strong>conservative<\/strong> if its\nwork depends only on the endpoints. Then a potential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexists such that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf F=-\\nabla U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{cons}}=-\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf all relevant forces are conservative,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=K+U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. With nonconservative work, instead\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta(K+U)=W_{\\mathrm{nc}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPower is the rate of doing work:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\mathrm dW/\\mathrm dt=\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the instantaneous force.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Spring and Turning Points<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal spring follows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=-kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has potential energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(x)=\\tfrac12 kx^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis displaced by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a spring with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=200\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand released from rest. Neglecting friction,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\tfrac12kA^2=1.00\\,\\mathrm J.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At equilibrium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe potential is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12mv_{\\max}^2=E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\max}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the turning points\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\pm A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nvelocity is zero. On a potential-energy diagram, the horizontal\ntotal-energy line intersects the potential at precisely those points.\nTotal energy stays constant even while it alternates between kinetic and\npotential forms.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_273_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parabolic spring potential with a horizontal energy line and two turning points\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parabolic spring potential with a\nhorizontal energy line and two turning points<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Turning points occur where the spring potential equals total\nenergy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A constant force of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts parallel to a displacement of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind its work.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmass falls from rest through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout drag. Find its speed just before reaching the ground.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the spring example, find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.05\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the remaining kinetic energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=Fd=30\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mgh=\\tfrac12mv^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{2gh}\\approx9.90\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.05<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\tfrac12\\cdot200\\cdot0.05^2=0.25\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1.00-0.25=0.75\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Friction and the Choice of System<\/h2>\n<p>Mechanical energy is not always conserved. Sliding friction converts\nsome of it into internal energy of the interacting bodies. The\n<strong>system boundary<\/strong> is therefore crucial: total energy\nremains conserved for a suitably closed system even as the mechanical\ncomponent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K+U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases. The zero of a potential may be chosen freely; physically\nrelevant quantities are potential differences. A potential minimum\ncorresponds to locally stable equilibrium when the remaining model\nassumptions hold.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Work transfers energy; conservative forces admit a potential\ndescription. For collisions, momentum provides another key tool.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Momentum, Collisions and the\nCentre of Mass<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-1-potential-energy-of-a-system\">University Physics Volume 1 – 8.1 Potential Energy of a System<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 8.1, introduction and learning objectives; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L4 – Work, Energy and Potential · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/8-4-potential-energy-diagrams-and-stability\">University Physics Volume 1 – 8.4 Potential Energy Diagrams and Stability<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 8.4, introduction; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L4 – Work, Energy and Potential · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-introduction\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 7 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · chapter outline and introduction; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L4 – Work, Energy and Potential · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Momentum, Collisions and the Centre of Mass<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Newtonian dynamics, energy and vector\ncalculations. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use momentum conservation in\ncollisions and relate centre-of-mass motion to external forces.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Momentum Simplifies Collisions<\/h2>\n<p>Collisions involve large forces over short intervals, making the\ndetailed time dependence difficult to measure. We instead study momentum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒑<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf p=m\\mathbf v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand define a system boundary. For a system of interacting bodies, pairs\nof <strong>internal<\/strong> forces cancel in the sum under the\nNewtonian interaction model. Consequently the change in total momentum\nis set by the external impulse. This can relate velocities before and\nafter a collision without resolving the details of the contact\nforce.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Impulse–Momentum Theorem and Centre of Mass<\/h2>\n<p>For the total momentum,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf P=\\sum_i m_i\\mathbf v_i,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\mathrm d\\mathbf P}{\\mathrm dt}=\\sum\\mathbf F_{\\mathrm{ext}},\\qquad\n\\Delta\\mathbf P=\\int\\sum\\mathbf F_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\,\\mathrm dt.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the external impulse is negligible during the collision,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis conserved. With constant total mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=\\sum_i m_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe centre of mass is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑹<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>M<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>M<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf R_{\\mathrm{CM}}=\\frac{\\sum_i m_i\\mathbf r_i}{M},\\qquad\nM\\mathbf a_{\\mathrm{CM}}=\\sum\\mathbf F_{\\mathrm{ext}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Conserved momentum does not automatically imply conserved kinetic\nenergy. In a perfectly inelastic collision the bodies move together\nafterwards, while some mechanical energy becomes deformation or internal\nenergy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Two Carts Stick Together<\/h2>\n<p>A cart of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_1=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhits a stationary cart of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_2=1.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a straight track. They lock together after impact. Neglecting\nexternal impulse,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_f=\\frac{m_1v_1+m_2v_2}{m_1+m_2}\n=\\frac{6.0\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}}{3.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}}\n=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Initially\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>3.0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_i=\\tfrac12\\cdot2.0\\cdot3.0^2=9.0\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nafterwards\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>2.0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_f=\\tfrac12\\cdot3.0\\cdot2.0^2=6.0\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Three joules of mechanical energy<\/strong> have been converted\nto other forms while total momentum remains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.0\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_134_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two carts before collision and a coupled pair afterwards with velocities\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two carts before collision and a coupled\npair afterwards with velocities<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Momentum is 6 kg m per second on both sides; kinetic energy falls\nfrom 9 to 6 joules.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncart travels at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind its momentum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two equal-mass carts with velocities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstick together. Find their final velocity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the main example, which energy quantity is not conserved, and by\nhow much does it change?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=mv=8\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Initial total momentum is zero, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_f=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Kinetic energy drops from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>9<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na loss of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the mechanical-energy account. Total energy is not destroyed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What Conservation Does Not Claim<\/h2>\n<p>In real experiments, friction and external impulses may change system\nmomentum; isolation is often only approximate during a brief collision.\nFor a two-dimensional collision, momentum must be balanced separately in\neach spatial direction. An “elastic collision” additionally requires\nequal total kinetic energy of the bodies before and after impact. It\ndoes not imply that no deformation occurs during contact.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A well-defined system allows a short momentum balance alongside a\nseparate energy check. Next comes rotational motion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Rotation, Torque and Moment of\nInertia<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-introduction\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 9 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter Outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L5 – Momentum, Collisions and the Centre of Mass · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M3-L6 – Rotation, Torque and Moment of Inertia<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Forces, circular motion, work and\nenergy. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model rotational motion with\nmoment of inertia and torque and apply the rolling constraint.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Angular Rather Than Linear Quantities<\/h2>\n<p>For rigid rotation about a fixed axis, the angular position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nangular velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\mathrm d\\varphi/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand angular acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=\\mathrm d\\omega/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribe motion. A point at distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the axis moves at speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=r\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPoints farther from the axis therefore move faster at the same angular\nvelocity. <strong>Torque<\/strong> depends on both force and lever arm: a\ntangential force produces more torque than an equal radial force at the\nsame point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Torque, Inertia and Energy Balance<\/h2>\n<p>The torque of a force about a reference point is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝝉<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><msubsup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>I<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol\\tau=\\mathbf r\\times\\mathbf F,\\qquad\nI=\\int r_\\perp^2\\,\\mathrm dm,\\qquad\nK_{\\mathrm{rot}}=\\tfrac12 I\\omega^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a rigid body rotating about a fixed principal axis with constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum\\tau=I\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMore generally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝝉<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum\\boldsymbol\\tau_{\\mathrm{ext}}=\\mathrm d\\mathbf L/\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMoment of inertia is not a universal number attached to a mass: it\ndepends on the distribution of mass relative to the chosen axis. For a\nuniform solid disk about its symmetry axis,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\tfrac12MR^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{\\mathrm{rot}}=\\tfrac14MR^2\\omega^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Torque and Acceleration<\/h2>\n<p>A tangential force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=3.0\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts at the rim of a uniform disk with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIgnore bearing friction. The torque is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=RF=0.60\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe moment of inertia about the centre is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>τ<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\tfrac12MR^2=0.040\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2},\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac{\\tau}{I}=15\\,\\mathrm{rad/s^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Starting from rest, the disk reaches angular velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile the force remains constant. A body rolling without slipping also\nsatisfies the kinematic condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{CM}}=R\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not imply constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin every rolling motion.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_252_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circular disk with radius, tangential force and torque direction\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Circular disk with radius, tangential\nforce and torque direction<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Tangential force at the rim generates torque about the disk\naxis.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A tangential force of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom an axis. Find the torque.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the moment of inertia of a uniform solid disk with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=4\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A wheel with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.25\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrolls without slipping at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{CM}}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=rF=1.5\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=MR^2/2=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=v_{\\mathrm{CM}}/R=8.0\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why the Axis Matters<\/h2>\n<p>Torques and angular momenta must refer to the same origin or axis;\notherwise balances become inconsistent. For a moving axis or asymmetric\nrotation, the scalar formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=I\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be insufficient; one must use the vector angular-momentum equation\nand sometimes the inertia tensor. Real wheels also experience\ndeformation, rolling resistance and occasional slip, none of which are\nincluded in the ideal rolling constraint.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Torque is the rotational counterpart of force, while moment of\ninertia measures resistance to angular acceleration. Gravitation follows\nnext.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Gravitation and Keplerian\nOrbits<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/11-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 11 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 11.1, Rolling Motion; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L6 – Rotation, Torque and Moment of Inertia · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/10-introduction\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 10 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter Outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L6 – Rotation, Torque and Moment of Inertia · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M3-L7 – Gravitation and Keplerian Orbits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Circular motion, potential energy and\nconservation of energy. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive and apply gravitational\nforce, circular-orbit speed and Kepler’s orbital laws.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Central Force and Orbit Shape<\/h2>\n<p>Newtonian gravitation is an attractive interaction between masses. In\nthe point-mass model, the force acts along the line joining the bodies\nand falls with the square of their separation. A small satellite outside\nan approximately spherically symmetric central mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be treated using the point-mass result. A <strong>circular\norbit<\/strong> is a special case; bound Kepler orbits are generally\nellipses, with the central mass at one focus. Force and orbital velocity\ncontinuously change their vector directions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gravity, Potential and Circular Orbit<\/h2>\n<p>For masses separated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Newtonian model gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑭<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>r<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf F|=G\\frac{Mm}{r^2},\\qquad\nU(r)=-G\\frac{Mm}{r},\\qquad\nv_{\\mathrm{circ}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{r}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Circular speed follows by equating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mv^2/r=GMm/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand presumes an ideal circular orbit. With potential zero at infinity\nand neglecting drag, the escape condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12mv^2-GMm/r\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{esc}}=\\sqrt{2GM/r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA negative value of gravitational potential energy does\n<strong>not<\/strong> imply negative total energy in every situation; the\nzero has been chosen to make binding meaningful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Circular Orbit Around Earth<\/h2>\n<p>Use Earth’s gravitational parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.986<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">GM\\approx3.986\\cdot10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{m^3/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an orbital radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=7.00\\cdot10^6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom Earth’s centre. A small satellite has<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>3.986<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>7.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.55<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{\\frac{3.986\\cdot10^{14}}{7.00\\cdot10^6}}\n\\approx7.55\\cdot10^3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Its orbital period is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.83<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi r/v\\approx5.83\\cdot10^3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>97.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">97.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nminutes. Kepler’s third law for the same central mass reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=4\\pi^2a^3/(GM)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the semimajor axis of an elliptical orbit. The drawing shows the\ncentral mass at a focus of an ellipse, not at its centre.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_139_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptical orbit with central body at a focus, periapsis and apoapsis\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptical orbit with central body at a\nfocus, periapsis and apoapsis<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The semimajor axis describes the orbit; the central body is not\nat the centre.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Double the radius of a circular orbit around the same central body.\nHow does speed change?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive the ratio of escape speed to circular speed.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What orbital quantity replaces circle radius in Kepler’s third law\nfor an ellipse?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\propto r^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nspeed becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof its previous value.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{esc}}/v_{\\mathrm{circ}}=\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The semimajor axis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a circle,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of the Two-Body Model<\/h2>\n<p>The Kepler model assumes an isolated two-body interaction with\npointlike or suitably spherically symmetric masses. Real satellite\norbits are perturbed by drag, Earth’s oblateness and other gravitating\nbodies. Relativistic corrections can be necessary in very strong gravity\nor for high precision. The escape-speed expression assumes no subsequent\npropulsion and neglects those other influences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Central gravity determines orbital speed and Kepler’s laws in the\ntwo-body model. Material deformation closes this module.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Stress, Strain and Linear\nElasticity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-5-keplers-laws-of-planetary-motion\">University Physics Volume 1 – 13.5 Kepler’s Laws of Planetary Motion<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 13.5, Kepler’s First Law and learning objectives; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L7 – Gravitation and Keplerian Orbits · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-key-equations\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 13 Key Equations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Formeltabelle, lines 22–33; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L7 – Gravitation and Keplerian Orbits · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M3-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M3-L8 – Stress, Strain and Linear Elasticity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Bachelor: Classical Mechanics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Force balance, geometry and SI units.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define stress and strain and\napply Hooke’s law to elastic bars.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Rigid to Deformable Bodies<\/h2>\n<p>Earlier models often treated objects as rigid. Real materials change\nshape under load. For a straight bar under axial tension, <strong>normal\nstress<\/strong> measures axial force per cross-sectional area and\n<strong>strain<\/strong> measures relative change in length. Both are\nneeded: the same tensile force stresses a thin bar more than a thick\none, and the same extension is a larger fraction of the length of a\nshort bar. Linear elasticity describes small reversible deformations,\nnot the full response up to fracture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stress, Strain and Young’s Modulus<\/h2>\n<p>For a uniform bar undergoing small axial deformation, the engineering\nquantities are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>F<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=\\frac{F}{A},\\qquad\n\\varepsilon=\\frac{\\Delta L}{L_0},\\qquad\n\\sigma=E\\varepsilon.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Stress\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of pascals\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pa}=\\mathrm{N/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>);\nstrain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis dimensionless, so Young’s modulus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso has units of pascals. Hooke’s law gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta L=\\frac{FL_0}{EA},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>provided\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be treated as approximately uniform along the bar. This uniaxial\nlinear model assumes a homogeneous material, small strains and loading\nwithin the elastic range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Extension of a Metal Bar<\/h2>\n<p>A bar has initial length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_0=2.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand cross-section\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=100\\,\\mathrm{mm^2}=1.00\\cdot10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is pulled axially with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=10\\,\\mathrm{kN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a material with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=200\\,\\mathrm{GPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>11<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=\\frac{10^4}{10^{-4}}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=100\\,\\mathrm{MPa},\\qquad\n\\varepsilon=\\frac{10^8}{2\\cdot10^{11}}=5.0\\cdot10^{-4}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The extension is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta L=\\varepsilon L_0=1.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram illustrates the initially linear relation between stress and\nstrain; outside that range the material may behave differently. The\nactual strength limit of a specific material has <strong>not<\/strong>\nbeen determined here.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_286_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Stress–strain graph with an initial linear-elastic region\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Stress–strain graph with an initial\nlinear-elastic region<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The initial linear slope is Young’s modulus E; behaviour beyond\nit is material dependent.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An axial force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=4.0\\,\\mathrm{kN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacts on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind normal stress.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbar extends by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind strain.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot equal the maximum allowable stress?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=2.0\\cdot10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=4.0\\cdot10^3/(2.0\\cdot10^{-4})=20\\,\\mathrm{MPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0005<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.0005/1.0=5.0\\cdot10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the slope of the linearly elastic stress–strain relationship;\nstrength is a separate property.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Material Limits and Other Loading Modes<\/h2>\n<p>The linear model does not predict fracture or permanent plastic\ndeformation. Higher loads may cause yielding, cracking, time-dependent\ncreep or buckling; temperature and loading history can matter as well.\nTension, compression, shear and volumetric compression involve different\nmeasures and material constants. General three-dimensional stress states\nrequire tensor descriptions. Exercises should therefore specify\ncross-section, load direction and the assumed validity range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Forces produce stress and strain in deformable bodies. Linear\nelasticity relates them only within a limited material range.<\/p>\n<p>Subsequent modules deepen mechanics and introduce other areas of\nphysics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-3-stress-strain-and-elastic-modulus\">University Physics Volume 1 – 12.3 Stress, Strain, and Elastic Modulus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 12.3, introduction; Subject relevance to lesson: M3-L8 – Stress, Strain and Linear Elasticity · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
